lly equivalent to $B.$" 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186158579 1064 "Two statements are **logically equivalent** if they have identical truth values in their respective truth tables. In this lesson, we'll use truth tables to show that two complex compound statements are logically equivalent. \n\nIf two statements $P$ and $Q$ are logically equivalent, we write\n\n$$\nP\\equiv Q\n$$\n\nwhere the symbol \"$\\equiv$\" means \"is logically equivalent to.\"\n\nFor example, let's use a truth table to prove the following logical equivalence:\n\n$$\n(A \\land \\neg A) \\lor B \\equiv B\n$$\n\nFirst, we construct truth tables with columns $A$ and $B$ containing all possible combinations of truth values. Then, we create another column for $\\neg A,$ which has the opposite truth values to $A.$\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n\nNow, we add a column for $A \\land \\neg A.$ Recall that $A\\land \\neg A$ is true if $A$ is true and $\\neg A$ is true and is false otherwise. In this case, $A\\land \\neg A$ is always false.\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\,$ | $\\, A \\land \\neg A \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n\nFinally, we complete the table with a column for $(A \\land \\neg A) \\lor B.$ Recall that $(A \\land \\neg A) \\lor B$ is true if $(A \\land \\neg A)$ is true, or $B$ is true, or both are true. \n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\,$ | $\\, A \\land \\neg A \\,$ | $\\, (A \\land \\neg A) \\lor B \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n\nWe see that the truth values of $(A \\land \\neg A) \\lor B$ are the same as the truth values of $B.$ This proves that $(A \\land \\neg A) \\lor B$ is logically equivalent to $B.$" 13194139540364 17592186576411 false] [17592186158586 1064 "The **associative law for disjunctions** is similar:\n\n$$\n(A \\lor B) \\lor C \\equiv A \\lor (B \\lor C)\n$$\n\nWe can prove this equivalence using a truth table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,A\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,B\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,C\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, A \\lor B\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, B \\lor C\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, (A \\lor B) \\lor C\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, A\\lor (B \\lor C)\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm T$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm T$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm T$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm T$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm T$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm T$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm T$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm F$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm T$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm T$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm T$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm F$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm T$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm T$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm T$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm T$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm F$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm F$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm T$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm F$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm F$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm T$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm T$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm T$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm T$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm F$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm T$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm F$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm T$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm T$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm F$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm F$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm T$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm F$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm T$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm F$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm F$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm F$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm F$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm F$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nIn natural language, the associative law for disjunctions amounts to moving a comma between \"or\" statements. For example, the associative law states that the sentence\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{The cat is quiet}}_{(A} \\quad \\underbrace{\\textrm{or}\\vphantom{p}}_{\\lor} \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{the dog is loud}}_{B)} \\quad , \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{or}}_{\\lor} \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{the turtle is slow}}_{C}\n$$\n\nis logically equivalent to the sentence\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{The cat is quiet}}_{A} \\quad ,  \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{or}}_{\\lor} \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{the dog is loud}}_{(B} \\quad \\underbrace{\\textrm{or}\\vphantom{p}}_{\\lor} \\quad \\underbrace{\\textrm{the turtle is slow}}_{C)}.\n$$" 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186158586 1064 "The **associative law for disjunctions** is similar:\n\n$$\n(A \\lor B) \\lor C \\equiv A \\lor (B \\lor C)\n$$\n\nWe can prove this equivalence using a truth table.\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,C\\,$ | $\\, A \\lor B\\,$ | $\\, B \\lor C\\,$ | $\\, (A \\lor B) \\lor C\\,$ | $\\, A\\lor (B \\lor C)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n\nIn natural language, the associative law for disjunctions amounts to moving a comma between \"or\" statements. For example, the associative law states that the sentence\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{The cat is quiet}}_{(A} \\quad \\underbrace{\\textrm{or}\\vphantom{p}}_{\\lor} \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{the dog is loud}}_{B)} \\quad , \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{or}}_{\\lor} \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{the turtle is slow}}_{C}\n$$\n\nis logically equivalent to the sentence\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{The cat is quiet}}_{A} \\quad ,  \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{or}}_{\\lor} \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{the dog is loud}}_{(B} \\quad \\underbrace{\\textrm{or}\\vphantom{p}}_{\\lor} \\quad \\underbrace{\\textrm{the turtle is slow}}_{C)}.\n$$" 13194139540364 17592186576411 false] [17592186158587 1064 "The **commutative law for disjunctions** is similar:\n\n$$\nA \\lor B \\equiv B \\lor A\n$$\n\nWe can prove this result using a truth table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,A\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,B\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,A \\lor B\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,B \\lor A\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm T$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm T$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm T$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm F$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm F$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm T$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm F$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm F$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLikewise, in natural language, the commutative law for disjunctions amounts to swapping two phrases surrounding the word \"or.\" For example, the commutative law states that the sentence\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{The cat is quiet}}_{(A} \\quad \\underbrace{\\textrm{or}\\vphantom{p}}_{\\lor} \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{the dog is loud}}_{B)}\n$$\n\nis logically equivalent to the sentence\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{The dog is loud}}_{(B} \\quad \\underbrace{\\textrm{or}\\vphantom{p}}_{\\lor} \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{the cat is quiet}}_{A)}.\n$$" 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186158587 1064 "The **commutative law for disjunctions** is similar:\n\n$$\nA \\lor B \\equiv B \\lor A\n$$\n\nWe can prove this result using a truth table.\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A \\lor B\\,$ | $\\,B \\lor A\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n\nLikewise, in natural language, the commutative law for disjunctions amounts to swapping two phrases surrounding the word \"or.\" For example, the commutative law states that the sentence\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{The cat is quiet}}_{(A} \\quad \\underbrace{\\textrm{or}\\vphantom{p}}_{\\lor} \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{the dog is loud}}_{B)}\n$$\n\nis logically equivalent to the sentence\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{The dog is loud}}_{(B} \\quad \\underbrace{\\textrm{or}\\vphantom{p}}_{\\lor} \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{the cat is quiet}}_{A)}.\n$$" 13194139540364 17592186576411 false] [17592186158610 1064 "Suppose we encrypt the $3$-letter English word $\\text{DOG}$ to ciphertext using the RSA cipher with the public key\n\n$$\n(e,n)=(13,51)\n$$\n\nWe'll assume that we use the usual English a