92186576411 false] [17592186161670 1071 "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFind the conditional probability mass function $f_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\, 1)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161670 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ | $0$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.1$ | $0$ | $0.2$ |\n\nFind the conditional probability mass function $f_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\, 1)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139540364 17592186576411 false] [17592186161670 1074 "The conditional probability mass function $f_{X|Y}(x|y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y) = P(X = x \\,|\\, Y=y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the following set of conditional probabilities:\n\n$$\nf_{X|Y}(x \\,|\\, 1) = P(X = x \\,|\\, Y=1)\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(X = x \\,|\\, Y=1) = \\dfrac{P(X = x, Y=1)}{P(Y=1)} = \\dfrac{f(x,1)}{f_Y(1)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and $f_Y(y)$ is the marginal distribution of $Y.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distributions for $X$ and $Y$ correspond to the row and column totals, respectively. So, let's compute these:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=5$ | $f_X(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ | $0$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.1$ | $0$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |\n| $f_Y(y)$ | $0.5$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, for $x\\in \\{0,2,4\\},$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle P ( X = 0 \\hspace{0.167em} | \\hspace{0.167em} Y = 1 ) & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{f ( 0 , 1 )}{ {f}_{Y} ( 1 )}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{0.1}{0.5}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{5}} \\\\ \\displaystyle P ( X = 2 \\hspace{0.167em} | \\hspace{0.167em} Y = 1 ) & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{f ( 2 , 1 )}{ {f}_{Y} ( 1 )}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{0.3}{0.5}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{5}} \\\\ \\displaystyle P ( X = 4 \\hspace{0.167em} | \\hspace{0.167em} Y = 1 ) & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{f ( 4 , 1 )}{ {f}_{Y} ( 1 )}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{0.1}{0.5}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{5}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nFinally, the conditional probability mass function can be represented by the following table:   \n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\, 1)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac 1 5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{3}{5}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac 1 5$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161670 1074 "The conditional probability mass function $f_{X|Y}(x|y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y) = P(X = x \\,|\\, Y=y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the following set of conditional probabilities:\n\n$$\nf_{X|Y}(x \\,|\\, 1) = P(X = x \\,|\\, Y=1)\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(X = x \\,|\\, Y=1) = \\dfrac{P(X = x, Y=1)}{P(Y=1)} = \\dfrac{f(x,1)}{f_Y(1)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and $f_Y(y)$ is the marginal distribution of $Y.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distributions for $X$ and $Y$ correspond to the row and column totals, respectively. So, let's compute these:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=5$ | $f_X(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ | $0$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.1$ | $0$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |\n| $f_Y(y)$ | $0.5$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, for $x\\in \\{0,2,4\\},$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle P ( X = 0 \\hspace{0.167em} | \\hspace{0.167em} Y = 1 ) & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{f ( 0 , 1 )}{ {f}_{Y} ( 1 )}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{0.1}{0.5}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{5}} \\\\ \\displaystyle P ( X = 2 \\hspace{0.167em} | \\hspace{0.167em} Y = 1 ) & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{f ( 2 , 1 )}{ {f}_{Y} ( 1 )}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{0.3}{0.5}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{5}} \\\\ \\displaystyle P ( X = 4 \\hspace{0.167em} | \\hspace{0.167em} Y = 1 ) & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{f ( 4 , 1 )}{ {f}_{Y} ( 1 )}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{0.1}{0.5}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{5}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nFinally, the conditional probability mass function can be represented by the following table:   \n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\, 1)$ | $\\dfrac 1 5$ | $\\dfrac{3}{5}$ | $\\dfrac 1 5$ |" 13194139540364 17592186576411 false] [17592186161671 1071 "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{6}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{9}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{6}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{18}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{18}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{6}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{9}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{9}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{18}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nCompute $f_{X|Y}(2 \\,|\\, 2)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161671 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\dfrac{1}{18}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{18}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{18}$ |\n\nCompute $f_{X|Y}(2 \\,|\\, 2)$ given that the d