| $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f_{Y|X}(y \\,|\\, 2)$ | $\\dfrac{2}{3}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ |\n\nFind the $\\textrm{E}\\big[Y \\,|\\, X=2\\big]$ given that the conditional probability mass function $f_{Y|X}(y \\,|\\, 2)$ is shown in the table above." 13194139540364 17592186576411 false] [17592186161690 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.05$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.05$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F\\left(2,1\\right).$" 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161690 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=-2$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F\\left(2,1\\right).$" 13194139540364 17592186576411 false] [17592186161690 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = P(X \\leq x, Y \\leq y) = \\sum\\limits_{x_i \\leq x} \\sum\\limits_{y_i \\leq y} f(x_i, y_i).\n$$\n\nBy definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,1) & =P(X\\le 2,Y\\le 1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by highlighting all cells in the table that correspond to $X \\leq 2, Y \\leq 1.$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FCF3CF\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FCF3CF\">$0.3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FCF3CF\">$0.05$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.05$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,1) & ={\\sum }_{x\\le 2}{\\sum }_{y\\le 1}f(x,y) \\\\  & =f(1,-2)+f(1,-1)+f(1,0) \\\\  & =0.1+0.3+0.05 \\\\  & =0.45.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161690 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = P(X \\leq x, Y \\leq y) = \\sum\\limits_{x_i \\leq x} \\sum\\limits_{y_i \\leq y} f(x_i, y_i).\n$$\n\nBy definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,1) & =P(X\\le 2,Y\\le 1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by highlighting all cells in the table that correspond to $X \\leq 2, Y \\leq 1.$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=-2$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,1) & ={\\sum }_{x\\le 2}{\\sum }_{y\\le 1}f(x,y) \\\\  & =f(1,-2)+f(1,-1)+f(1,0) \\\\  & =0.1+0.3+0.05 \\\\  & =0.45.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139540364 17592186576411 false] [17592186161692 1071 "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{9}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{2}{9}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{8}{27}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{5}{27}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{2}{27}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{9}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFind $\\textrm{E}\\big[Y \\,|\\, X=2\\big]$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161692 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{9}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\dfrac{8}{27}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{27}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\dfrac{2}{27}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ |\n\nFind $\\textrm{E}\\big[Y \\,|\\, X=2\\big]$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139540364 17592186576411 false] [17592186161692 1074 "Recall that the conditional expected value of  $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\big[Y \\,|\\, X=x\\big] = \\sum_{y} y \\, f_{Y | X}(y \\,|\\, x).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $f_{Y | X}(y \\,|\\, x),$ let's first find the marginal distribution for $X,$ corresponding to the row totals: \n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f_X(x)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{9}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{2}{9}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$\\dfrac{1}{3}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{8}{27}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{5}{27}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$\\dfrac{13}{27}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{2}{27}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{9}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$\\dfrac{5}{27}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x$ is\n\n$$\nf_{Y | X}(y \\,|\\, x)  = \\dfrac{f(x,y)}{f_X(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding row total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x{:}$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f_{Y | X}(y \\,|\\, x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{3}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{2}{3}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{8}{13}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{5}{13}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{2}{5}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{3}{5}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=2] & ={\\sum }_{y}y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{8}{13}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161692 1074 "Recall that the conditional expected value of  $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\big[Y \\,|\\, X=x\\big] = \\sum_{y} y \\, f_{Y | X}(y \\,|\\, x).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $f_{Y | X}(y \\,|\\, x),$ let's first find the marginal distribution for $X,$ corresponding to the row totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $f_X(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\dfrac{8}{27}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{27}$ | $\\dfrac{13}{27}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\dfrac{2}{27}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{27}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x$ is\n\n$$\nf_{Y | X}(y \\,|\\, x)  = \\dfrac{f(x,y)}{f_X(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding row total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x{:}$\n\n| $f_{Y | X}(y \\,|\\, x)$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{3}$ | $1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\dfrac{8}{13}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{13}$ | $1$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\dfrac{2}{5}$ | $\\dfrac{3}{5}$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=2] & ={\\sum }_{y}y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{8}{13}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139540364 17592186576411 false] [17592186161698 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nCalculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161698 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.5$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0$ | $0.4$ |\n\nCalculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" 13194139540364 17592186576411 false] [17592186161698 1074 "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] = \\sum_{(x,y)\\in S}(x − \\textrm E[X])(y − \\textrm E[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f_X$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$0.6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$0.4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f_Y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$0.9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe now compute $\\textrm{E}[X]$ and $\\textrm{E}[Y],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.6+2\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =0.6+0.8 \\\\  & =1.4 \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.1+1\\cdot 0.9 \\\\  & =0+0.9 \\\\  & =0.9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the covariance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-1.4)(y-0.9)f(x,y) \\\\  & =(1-1.4)(0-0.9)f(1,0) \\\\  & +(1-1.4)(1-0.9)f(1,1) \\\\  & +(2-1.4)(0-0.9)f(2,0) \\\\  & +(2-1.4)(1-0.9)f(2,1) \\\\  & =(-0.4)(-0.9)(0.1) \\\\  & +(-0.4)(0.1)(0.5) \\\\  & +(0.6)(-0.9)(0) \\\\  & +(0.6)(0.1)(0.4) \\\\  & =0.04.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161698 1074 "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] = \\sum_{(x,y)\\in S}(x − \\textrm E[X])(y − \\textrm E[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.5$ | $0.6$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0$ | $0.4$ | $0.4$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $0.1$ | $0.9$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now compute $\\textrm{E}[X]$ and $\\textrm{E}[Y],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.6+2\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =0.6+0.8 \\\\  & =1.4 \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.1+1\\cdot 0.9 \\\\  & =0+0.9 \\\\  & =0.9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the covariance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-1.4)(y-0.9)f(x,y) \\\\  & =(1-1.4)(0-0.9)f(1,0) \\\\  & +(1-1.4)(1-0.9)f(1,1) \\\\  & +(2-1.4)(0-0.9)f(2,0) \\\\  & +(2-1.4)(1-0.9)f(2,1) \\\\  & =(-0.4)(-0.9)(0.1) \\\\  & +(-0.4)(0.1)(0.5) \\\\  & +(0.6)(-0.9)(0) \\\\  & +(0.6)(0.1)(0.4) \\\\  & =0.04.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139540364 17592186576411 false] [17592186161699 1071 "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{9}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{2}{9}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{3}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{9}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{9}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{9}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFind $\\textrm{E}\\big[X \\,|\\, Y=1\\big]$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161699 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{9}$ |\n| $X=4$ | $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ |\n| $X=6$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ |\n\nFind $\\textrm{E}\\big[X \\,|\\, Y=1\\big]$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139540364 17592186576411 false] [17592186161699 1074 "Recall that the conditional expected value of  $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\big[X \\,|\\, Y=y\\big] = \\sum_{x} x \\, f_{X | Y}(x \\,|\\, y).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $f_{X | Y}(x \\,|\\, y),$ let's first find the marginal distribution for $Y,$ corresponding to the column totals: \n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{9}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{2}{9}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{3}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{9}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{9}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{9}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f_Y(y)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$\\dfrac{5}{9}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$\\dfrac{4}{9}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y$ is\n\n$$\nf_{X | Y}(x \\,|\\, y)  = \\dfrac{f(x,y)}{f_Y(y)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding column total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y{:}$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f_{X | Y}(x \\,|\\, y)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{5}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{2}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{3}{5}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{5}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=1] & ={\\sum }_{x}x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,1) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}+4\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}+6\\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{5} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161699 1074 "Recall that the conditional expected value of  $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\big[X \\,|\\, Y=y\\big] = \\sum_{x} x \\, f_{X | Y}(x \\,|\\, y).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $f_{X | Y}(x \\,|\\, y),$ let's first find the marginal distribution for $Y,$ corresponding to the column totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{9}$ |\n| $X=4$ | $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ |\n| $X=6$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ |\n| $f_Y(y)$ | $\\dfrac{5}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{4}{9}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y$ is\n\n$$\nf_{X | Y}(x \\,|\\, y)  = \\dfrac{f(x,y)}{f_Y(y)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding column total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y{:}$\n\n| $f_{X | Y}(x \\,|\\, y)$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ |\n| $