l=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Screenplay</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Actors' performances</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$d_i$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$d_i^2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_i d_i = 0\n$$\n\nas expected, and\n\n$$\n\\sum_i d_i^2 = 4+16+4+4+4 = 32\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Spearman's rank correlation coefficient is\n\n$$\nr_s = 1 - \\dfrac{6(32)}{5(5^2-1)} = -0.6\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n-0.7 < r_s \\leq -0.3\n$$\n\nwe conclude that there is a **weak negative correlation** between the rankings." 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161731 1074 "The Spearman's rank correlation coefficient $r_s$ is Pearson's correlation coefficient applied to two sets of ranks.\n\nGiven two sets of ranks $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ and $y_1, y_2, \\ldots, y_n$, where the numbers in each set are uniquely expressed as integers between $1$ and $n$, the Spearman's rank correlation coefficient can 