/div>\n\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X > 0.989) = 0.995.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=0.989.$" 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161734 1074 "The table shows probabilities in the form $P(X > x).$ So first, we express the desired probability in this form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>x) & =1-P(X\\le x) \\\\  & =1-0.005 \\\\  & =0.995\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $X \\sim \\chi^2(7),$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=7.$\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $7$ | $\\,0.989\\,$ | $\\,1.239\\,$ | $\\,1.690\\,$ | $\\,2.167\\,$ | $\\,2.833\\,$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X > 0.989) = 0.995.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=0.989.$" 13194139540364 17592186576411 false] [17592186161735 1071 "Let the random variable $Y$ be defined as\n\n$$\nY = Z_1^2+Z_2^2+\\cdots + Z_{10}^2\n$$\n\nwhere $Z_i\\sim N(0,1)$ for $1\\leq i\\leq 10.$ Find the value $y$ that satisfies $P(Y\\leq y) = 0.99.$\n\n*Hint: The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\geq x) = p,$ where $X\\sim \\chi^2(k).$*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$k \\setminus p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.010$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.150$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.200$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$8$</td>\n<td>$\\,20.090\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,15.507\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,13.362\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,12.027\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,11.030\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$9$</td>\n<td>$\\,21.666\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,16.919\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,14.684\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,13.288\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,12.242\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$10$</td>\n<td>$\\,23.209\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,18.307\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,15.987\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,14.534\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,13.442\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161735 1071 "Let the random variable $Y$ be defined as\n\n$$\nY = Z_1^2+Z_2^2+\\cdots + Z_{10}^2\n$$\n\nwhere $Z_i\\sim N(0,1)$ for $1\\leq i\\leq 10.$ Find the value $y$ that satisfies $P(Y\\leq y) = 0.99.$\n\n*Hint: The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\geq x) = p,$ where $X\\sim \\chi^2(k).$*\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.010$ | $0.050$ | $0.100$ | $0.150$ | $0.200$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $8$ | $\\,20.090\\,$ | $\\,15.507\\,$ | $\\,13.362\\,$ | $\\,12.027\\,$ | $\\,11.030\\,$ |\n| $9$ | $\\,21.666\\,$ | $\\,16.919\\,$ | $\\,14.684\\,$ | $\\,13.288\\,$ | $\\,12.242\\,$ |\n| $10$ | $\\,23.209\\,$ | $\\,18.307\\,$ | $\\,15.987\\,$ | $\\,14.534\\,$ | $\\,13.442\\,$ |" 13194139540364 17592186576411 false] [17592186161735 1074 "If $Z_i\\sim N(0,1)$ for $1\\leq i \\leq k$ are independent normal random variables, then the sum\n\n$$\nZ_1^2+Z_2^2+\\cdots + Z_{k}^2\n$$\n\nfollows a $\\chi^2(k)$ distribution with $k$ degrees of freedom.\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\nY = Z_1^2+Z_2^2+\\cdots + Z_{10}^2 \\sim \\chi^2(10).\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the given probability in the form $P(Y\\geq y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le y) & =1-P(Y\\ge y) \\\\ 0.99 & =1-P(Y\\ge y) \\\\ P(Y\\ge y) & =0.01\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we wish to find the value $y$ such that $P(Y\\geq y) = 0.01$ This can be found from the given table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$k \\setminus p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.010$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.150$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.200$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$8$</td>\n<td>$\\,20.090\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,15.507\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,13.362\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,12.027\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,11.030\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$9$</td>\n<td>$\\,21.666\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,16.919\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,14.684\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,13.288\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,12.242\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$10$</td>\n<td>$\\,\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}23.209}\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,18.307\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,15.987\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,14.534\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,13.442\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y\\geq 23.209) = 0.01\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\nP(Y\\leq 23.209) = 0.99.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value $y$ that satisfies $P(Y\\leq y) = 0.99$ is $y=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}23.209}.$" 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161735 1074 "If $Z_i\\sim N(0,1)$ for $1\\leq i \\leq k$ are independent normal random variables, then the sum\n\n$$\nZ_1^2+Z_2^2+\\cdots + Z_{k}^2\n$$\n\nfollows a $\\chi^2(k)$ distribution with $k$ degrees of freedom.\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\nY = Z_1^2+Z_2^2+\\cdots + Z_{10}^2 \\sim \\chi^2(10).\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the given probability in the form $P(Y\\geq y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le y) & =1-P(Y\\ge y) \\\\ 0.99 & =1-P(Y\\ge y) \\\\ P(Y\\ge y) & =0.01\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we wish to find the value $y$ such that $P(Y\\geq y) = 0.01$ This can be found from the given table:\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.010$ | $0.050$ | $0.100$ | $0.150$ | $0.200$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $8$ | $\\,20.090\\,$ | $\\,15.507\\,$ | $\\,13.362\\,$ | $\\,12.027\\,$ | $\\,11.030\\,$ |\n| $9$ | $\\,21.666\\,$ | $\\,16.919\\,$ | $\\,14.684\\,$ | $\\,13.288\\,$ | $\\,12.242\\,$ |\n| $10$ | $\\,\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}23.209}\\,$ | $\\,18.307\\,$ | $\\,15.987\\,$ | $\\,14.534\\,$ | $\\,13.442\\,$ |\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y\\geq 23.209) = 0.01\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\nP(Y\\leq 23.209) = 0.99.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value $y$ that satisfies $P(Y\\leq y) = 0.99$ is $y=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}23.209}.$" 13194139540364 17592186576411 false] [17592186161736 1071 "Suppose $X$ is a random variable with support $S=\\{0,1,2,3\\}$. We wish to carry out a hypothesis test with the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\: X\\sim B(3, 0.4)$\n- $H_1:\\:X\\not\\sim B(3, 0.4)$\n\nA random sample is carried out, and the data is shown below. Calculate the expected frequency $E_i$ for each observation.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_i$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$O_i$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$120$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$180$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$65$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$35$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161736 1071 "Suppose $X$ is a random variable with support $S=\\{0,1,2,3\\}$. We wish to carry out a hypothesis test with the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\: X\\sim B(3, 0.4)$\n- $H_1:\\:X\\not\\sim B(3, 0.4)$\n\nA random sample is carried out, and the data is shown below. Calculate the expected frequency $E_i$ for each observation.\n\n| $x_i$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $O_i$ | $120$ | $180$ | $65$ | $35$ |" 13194139540364 17592186576411 false] [17592186161736 1074 "Under the null hypothesis, we have that $X\\sim B(3, 0.4)$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle P ( X = x ) & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x} \\right) {p}^{x} ( 1 - p {)}^{n - x} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{x} \\right) ( 0.4 {)}^{x} ( 0.6 {)}^{3 - x} , \\hspace{2em} x = 0 , 1 , 2 , 3.\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate the probability of each value of $X{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle P ( X = 0 ) & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{0} \\right) ( 0.4 {)}^{0} ( 0.6 {)}^{3} = 0.216 \\\\ \\displaystyle P ( X = 1 ) & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{1} \\right) ( 0.4 {)}^{1} ( 0.6 {)}^{2} = 0.432 \\\\ \\displaystyle P ( X = 2 ) & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{2} \\right) ( 0.4 {)}^{2} ( 0.6 {)}^{1} = 0.288 \\\\ \\displaystyle P ( X = 3 ) & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{3} \\right) ( 0.4 {)}^{3} ( 0.6 {)}^{0} = 0.064\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nThe sample size is\n\n$$\nN = 120+180+65+35 = 400\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected frequencies for each observation are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{0} &= N \\cdot P(X = 0) = 400 \\cdot 0.216 = 86.4 \\\\\nE_{1} &= N \\cdot P(X = 1) = 400 \\cdot 0.432 = 172.8 \\\\\nE_{2} &= N \\cdot P(X = 2) = 400 \\cdot 0.288 = 115.2 \\\\\nE_{3} &= N \\cdot P(X = 3) = 400 \\cdot 0.064 = 25.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA table summarizing the observed and corresponding expected frequencies is given below:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_i$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$O_i$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$120$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$180$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$65$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$35$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$E_i$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}86.4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}172.8}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}115.2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}25.6}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161736 1074 "Under the null hypothesis, we have that $X\\sim B(3, 0.4)$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle P ( X = x ) & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x} \\right) {p}^{x} ( 1 - p {)}^{n - x} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{x} \\right) ( 0.4 {)}^{x} ( 0.6 {)}^{3 - x} , \\hspace{2em} x = 0 , 1 , 2 , 3.\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate the probability of each value of $X{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle P ( X = 0 ) & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{0} \\right) ( 0.4 {)}^{0} ( 0.6 {)}^{3} = 0.216 \\\\ \\displaystyle P ( X = 1 ) & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{1} \\right) ( 0.4 {)}^{1} ( 0.6 {)}^{2} = 0.432 \\\\ \\displaystyle P ( X = 2 ) & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{2} \\right) ( 0.4 {)}^{2} ( 0.6 {)}^{1} = 0.288 \\\\ \\displaystyle P ( X = 3 ) & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{3} \\right) ( 0.4 {)}^{3} ( 0.6 {)}^{0} = 0.064\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nThe sample size is\n\n$$\nN = 120+180+65+35 = 400\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected frequencies for each observation are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{0} &= N \\cdot P(X = 0) = 400 \\cdot 0.216 = 86.4 \\\\\nE_{1} &= N \\cdot P(X = 1) = 400 \\cdot 0.432 = 172.8 \\\\\nE_{2} &= N \\cdot P(X = 2) = 400 \\cdot 0.288 = 115.2 \\\\\nE_{3} &= N \\cdot P(X = 3) = 400 \\cdot 0.064 = 25.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA table summarizing the observed and corresponding expected frequencies is given below:\n\n| $x_i$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $O_i$ | $120$ | $180$ | $65$ | $35$ |\n| $E_i$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}86.4}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}172.8}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}115.2}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}25.6}$ |" 13194139540364 17592186576411 false] [17592186161737 1071 "The manager of a chocolate shop believes that the weekly number of customer complaints about the service follows a Poisson distribution with rate parameter $\\lambda$. The frequency distribution of the complaints over $40$ weeks is summarized below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Number of Complaints $(y_i)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\geq3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Observed Frequencies $(O_i)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nConduct a chi-square goodness of fit test at the $10\\%$ significance level with the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\: Y\\sim \\mathrm{Po}(\\lambda)$\n- $H_1:\\: Y\\not\\sim \\mathrm{Po}(\\lambda)$\n\nwhere the rate parameter $\\lambda$ is unknown.\n\nThe table below gives the values of $w$ that satisfy $P(W\\geq w) = p$, where $W\\sim \\chi^2(\\nu)$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\nu \\setminus p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.010$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.100$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1$</td>\n<td>$\\,6.635\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,3.841\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.706\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$2$</td>\n<td>$\\,9.210\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,5.991\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,4.605\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$3$</td>\n<td>$\\,11.345\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,7.815\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,6.251\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161737 1071 "The manager of a chocolate shop believes that the weekly number of customer complaints about the service follows a Poisson distribution with rate parameter $\\lambda$. The frequency distribution of the complaints over $40$ weeks is summarized below.\n\n| Number of Complaints $(y_i)$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\geq3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Observed Frequencies $(O_i)$ | $20$ | $12$ | $8$ | $0$ |\n\nConduct a chi-square goodness of fit test at the $10\\%$ significance level with the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\: Y\\sim \\mathrm{Po}(\\lambda)$\n- $H_1:\\: Y\\not\\sim \\mathrm{Po}(\\lambda)$\n\nwhere the rate parameter $\\lambda$ is unknown.\n\nThe table below gives the values of $w$ that satisfy $P(W\\geq w) = p$, where $W\\sim \\chi^2(\\nu)$.\n\n| $\\nu \\setminus p$ | $0.010$ | $0.050$ | $0.100$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $\\,6.635\\,$ | $\\,3.841\\,$ | $\\,2.706\\,$ |\n| $2$ | $\\,9.210\\,$ | $\\,5.991\\,$ | $\\,4.605\\,$ |\n| $3$ | $\\,11.345\\,$ | $\\,7.815\\,$ | $\\,6.251\\,$ |" 13194139540364 17592186576411 false] [17592186161737 1074 "Suppose we have a set of observed frequencies $O_i$ and corresponding expected frequencies $E_i$, as defined by a theoretical model under the null hypothesis. Then, the random variable\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i} \\frac{(O_i - E_i)^2}{E_i}\n$$\n\napproximately follows a chi-square distribution with $\\nu$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $O_i$ is the observed frequency for category $i$, and\n- $E_i$ is the expected frequency for category $i$, as predicted under the null hypothesis.\n\nThis approximation holds when all expected frequencies $E_i$ are sufficiently large (typically\n\n$$\nE_i \\geq 5\n$$\n\nfor each category), and the observations are independent.\n\nLet $Y$ be the number of customer complaints received per week. Under the null hypothesis, we have that $Y \\sim \\mathrm{Po}(\\lambda)$.\n\nThe number of observations is\n\n$$\nN = 20+12+8 =40\n$$\n\nWe can calculate an estimator for $\\lambda$, which we denote as $\\hat \\lambda$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hat{λ} &= \\frac{total number of events}{total number of observations} \\\\\n&= (∑(y_{i} \\cdot O_{i}))/(N)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $O_i$ is the observed frequency for the $i$th observation, $N$ is the number of observations. So, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hat{λ} &= \\frac{0 \\cdot 20 + 1 \\cdot 12 + 2 \\cdot 8}{40} \\\\\n&= 0.7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the proposed distribution is $Y\\sim \\mathrm{Po}(0.7)$.\n\nUnder the hypothesis that $Y \\sim \\mathrm{Po}(0.7)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Y = y) &= \\frac{λ^{y}e^{- λ}}{y!} \\\\\n&= ((0.7)^{y}e^{-0.7})/(y!), y = 0, 1, 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate the probability of each value of $Y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Y = 0) &= ((0.7)^{0}e^{-0.7})/(0!) \\approx 0.496585 \\\\\nP(Y = 1) &= ((0.7)^{1}e^{-0.7})/(1!) \\approx 0.347610 \\\\\nP(Y = 2) &= ((0.7)^{2}e^{-0.7})/(2!) \\approx 0.121663 \\\\\nP(Y \\ge 3) &= 1 - P(Y = 0) - P(Y = 1) - P(Y = 2) = 0.034142\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected frequencies for each observation are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{0} &= N \\cdot P(Y = 0) = 40 \\cdot 0.496585 \\approx 19.8634 \\\\\nE_{1} &= N \\cdot P(Y = 1) = 40 \\cdot 0.347610 \\approx 13.9044 \\\\\nE_{2} &= N \\cdot P(Y = 2) = 40 \\cdot 0.121663 \\approx 4.8665 \\\\\nE_{3} &= N \\cdot P(Y \\ge 3) = 40 \\cdot 0.034142 \\approx 1.3657\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that there are two frequencies less than $5$, so we combine the values $Y = 2$ and $Y=3$.\n\nA table summarizing the observed and corresponding theoretical frequencies is given below:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_i$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\geq2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$O_i$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$E_i$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$19.8634$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13.9044$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6.2322$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\dfrac{(O_i-E_i)^2}{E_i}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.0009$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2608$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.5014$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe have rounded values to four decimal places, where appropriate.\n\nWe compute our test statistic by summing the last row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(i)((O_{i} - E_{i})^{2})/(E_{i}) &= 0.0009 + 0.2608 + 0.5014 \\\\\n&\\approx 0.8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the test statistic is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.8}$ (rounded to one decimal place).\n\nOur data has two constraints: the total number of observations must equal $N$, and the estimated rate $\\hat \\lambda$. Therefore, the number of degrees of freedom $\\nu$ is calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nν &= number of categories - number of constraints \\\\\n&= 3 - 2 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch Out!** We must use the number of categories in the combined table!\n\nSo, there is\n\n$$\n\\nu = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}\n$$\n\ndegree of freedom.\n\nFrom the given chi-square table, the critical value for $\\nu = 1$ at a $10\\%$ significance level is\n\n$$\n\\chi^2_{\\textrm{critical}} = 2.706\n$$\n\nand the critical region is\n\n$$\nX\\geq 2.706\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/15/155070611813bc121d3af67bb5e53c5504bfa3e089a4b0b1d491e8f351f36bc7.png)\n\nOur test statistic $(0.8)$ is not inside the critical region. Therefore, there is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{insufficient}}$ evidence to reject the null hypothesis." 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161737 1074 "Suppose we have a set of observed frequencies $O_i$ and corresponding expected frequencies $E_i$, as defined by a theoretical model under the null hypothesis. Then, the random variable\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i} \\frac{(O_i - E_i)^2}{E_i}\n$$\n\napproximately follows a chi-square distribution with $\\nu$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $O_i$ is the observed frequency for category $i$, and\n- $E_i$ is the expected frequenc