ter\">$8\\leq y_i < 16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1877$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}26.5}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16\\leq y_i < 24$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$47$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.3671$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}51.8}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24\\leq y_i < 32$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$53$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2936$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}41.4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$32\\leq y_i < 40$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.0958$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}13.5}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161739 1074 "The number of observations is\n\n$$\nN= 6+13+47+53+22= 141\n$$\n\nWe compute the expected frequencies for each class of observations by multiplying the total number of observations by the probability that $Y$ lies in that class.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{1} &= 141 \\cdot 0.0390 \\approx 5.5 \\\\\nE_{2} &= 141 \\cdot 0.1877 \\approx 26.5 \\\\\nE_{3} &= 141 \\cdot 0.3671 \\approx 51.8 \\\\\nE_{4} &= 141 \\cdot 0.2936 \\approx 41.4 \\\\\nE_{5} &= 141 \\cdot 0.0958 \\approx 13.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our completed table is as follows:\n\n| $y_i$ | $O_i$ | $P\\left(a < Y < b\\right)$ | $E_i$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0\\leq y_i < 8$ | $6$ | $0.0390$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}5.5}$ |\n| $8\\leq y_i < 16$ | $13$ | $0.1877$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}26.5}$ |\n| $16\\leq y_i < 24$ | $47$ | $0.3671$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}51.8}$ |\n| $24\\leq y_i < 32$ | $53$ | $0.2936$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}41.4}$ |\n| $32\\leq y_i < 40$ | $22$ | $0.0958$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}13.5}$ |" 13194139540364 17592186576411 false] [17592186161740 1071 "Suppose $Y$ is a continuous random variable. We wish to conduct a chi-square goodness-of-fit test at the $2.5\\%$ significance level with the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\:$ $Y\\sim N(16,(\\sqrt{20})^2)$\n- $H_1:\\:$ $Y\\not\\sim N(16,(\\sqrt{20})^2)$\n\nA random sample is carried out, and the sample data is shown below. The rightmost column gives $P\\left(a < Y < b\\right)$ under the null hypothesis, where $a$ and $b$ are the boundaries for each class of observations.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Observations $(y_i)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency $(O_i)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$P\\left(a < Y < b\\right)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4\\leq y_i < 10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.0862$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10\\leq y_i < 16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$45$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.4101$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16\\leq y_i < 22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$78$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.4101$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22\\leq y_i < 28$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.0862$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nCalculate the value of the test statistic for this hypothesis test, rounded to one decimal place." 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161740 1071 "Suppose $Y$ is a continuous random variable. We wish to conduct a chi-square goodness-of-fit test at the $2.5\\%$ significance level with the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\:$ $Y\\sim N(16,(\\sqrt{20})^2)$\n- $H_1:\\:$ $Y\\not\\sim N(16,(\\sqrt{20})^2)$\n\nA random sample is carried out, and the sample data is shown below. The rightmost column gives $P\\left(a < Y < b\\right)$ under the null hypothesis, where $a$ and $b$ are the boundaries for each class of observations.\n\n| Observations $(y_i)$ | Frequency $(O_i)$ | $P\\left(a < Y < b\\right)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $4\\leq y_i < 10$ | $9$ | $0.0862$ |\n| $10\\leq y_i < 16$ | $45$ | $0.4101$ |\n| $16\\leq y_i < 22$ | $78$ | $0.4101$ |\n| $22\\leq y_i < 28$ | $18$ | $0.0862$ |\n\nCalculate the value of the test statistic for this hypothesis test, rounded to one decimal place." 13194139540364 17592186576411 false] [17592186161740 1074 "Suppose we have a set of observed frequencies $O_i$ and corresponding expected frequencies $E_i$, as defined by a theoretical model under the null hypothesis. Then, the random variable\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i} \\frac{(O_i - E_i)^2}{E_i}\n$$\n\napproximately follows a chi-square distribution with $\\nu$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $O_i$ is the observed frequency for category $i$, and\n- $E_i$ is the expected frequency for category $i$, as predicted under the null hypothesis.\n\nThis approximation holds when all expected frequencies $E_i$ are sufficiently large (typically\n\n$$\nE_i \\geq 5\n$$\n\nfor each category), and the observations are independent.\n\nThe number of observations is\n\n$$\nN= 9+45+78+18 = 150\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the expected frequencies for each class of observations.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_i$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$O_i$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$P\\left(a < Y < b\\right)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$E_i$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\dfrac{(O_i - E_i)^2}{E_i}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4\\leq y_i < 10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.0862$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12.9300$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.1945$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10\\leq y_i < 16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$45$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.4101$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$61.5150$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4.4338$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16\\leq y_i < 22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$78$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.4101$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$61.5150$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4.4177$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22\\leq y_i < 28$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.0862$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12.9300$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.9880$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNotice that all the expected frequencies are greater than or equal to $5$. We have rounded to four decimal places, where appropriate.\n\nFinally, we calculate the test statistic by summing the values in the last row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(i)((O_{i} - E_{i})^{2})/(E_{i}) &= 1.1945 + 4.4338 + 4.4177 + 1.9880 \\\\\n&\\approx 12.0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the test statistic is approximately $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}12.0}$." 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161740 1074 "Suppose we have a set of observed frequencies $O_i$ and corresponding expected frequencies $E_i$, as defined by a theoretical model under the null hypothesis. Then, the random variable\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i} \\frac{(O_i - E_i)^2}{E_i}\n$$\n\napproximately follows a chi-square distribution with $\\nu$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $O_i$ is the observed frequency for category $i$, and\n- $E_i$ is the expected frequency for category $i$, as predicted under the null hypothesis.\n\nThis approximation holds when all expected frequencies $E_i$ are sufficiently large (typically\n\n$$\nE_i \\geq 5\n$$\n\nfor each category), and the observations are independent.\n\nThe number of observations is\n\n$$\nN= 9+45+78+18 = 150\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the expected frequencies for each class of observations.\n\n| $y_i$ | $O_i$ | $P\\left(a < Y < b\\right)$ | $E_i$ | $\\dfrac{(O_i - E_i)^2}{E_i}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $4\\leq y_i < 10$ | $9$ | $0.0862$ | $12.9300$ | $1.1945$ |\n| $10\\leq y_i < 16$ | $45$ | $0.4101$ | $61.5150$ | $4.4338$ |\n| $16\\leq y_i < 22$ | $78$ | $0.4101$ | $61.5150$ | $4.4177$ |\n| $22\\leq y_i < 28$ | $18$ | $0.0862$ | $12.9300$ | $1.9880$ |\n\nNotice that all the expected frequencies are greater than or equal to $5$. We have rounded to four decimal places, where appropriate.\n\nFinally, we calculate the test statistic by summing the values in the last row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(i)((O_{i} - E_{i})^{2})/(E_{i}) &= 1.1945 + 4.4338 + 4.4177 + 1.9880 \\\\\n&\\approx 12.0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the test statistic is approximately $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}12.0}$." 13194139540364 17592186576411 false] [17592186161857 1071 "The probability distribution of the random variable $X$ is given below. Given that $\\textrm E[X] = 2.3,$ calculate $\\textrm{Var}[-10X].$ \n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"