n=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$a$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac 52a$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above. Find the value of $a.$" 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161863 1071 "|  | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\dfrac a2$ | $a$ |\n| $X=1$ | $a$ | $\\dfrac 52a$ |\n\nThe joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above. Find the value of $a.$" 13194139540364 17592186576411 false] [17592186161871 1071 "Given the joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ below for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ find $P(X = 0).$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{6}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{5}{12}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{6}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161871 1071 "Given the joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ below for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ find $P(X = 0).$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{12}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ |" 13194139540364 17592186576411 false] [17592186161871 1074 "If the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have joint distribution with joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & ={f}_{X}(x)={\\sum }_{y}f(x,y) \\\\ P(Y=y) & ={f}_{Y}(y)={\\sum }_{x}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y(y)$ are the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nIn this case, the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Let's add these row and column totals to our table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f_X$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{6}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{5}{12}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{7}{12}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{6}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$\\dfrac{5}{12}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f_Y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$\\dfrac{5}{12}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$\\dfrac{7}{12}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, using the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0) & ={f}_{X}(0) \\\\  & =f(0,0)+f(0,1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{5}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161871 1074 "If the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have joint distribution with joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & ={f}_{X}(x)={\\sum }_{y}f(x,y) \\\\ P(Y=y) & ={f}_{Y}(y)={\\sum }_{x}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y(y)$ are the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nIn this case, the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Let's add these row and column totals to our table:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{12}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{7}{12}}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{12}$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $\\dfrac{5}{12}$ | $\\dfrac{7}{12}$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, using the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0) & ={f}_{X}(0) \\\\  & =f(0,0)+f(0,1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{5}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139540364 17592186576411 false] [17592186161872 1071 "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.35$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.05$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nGiven the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ above for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ calculate $P(X \\geq 2).$" 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161872 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.35$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ |\n\nGiven the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ above for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ calculate $P(X \\geq 2).$" 13194139540364 17592186576411 false] [17592186161872 1074 "If the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have joint distribution with joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & ={f}_{X}(x)={\\sum }_{y}f(x,y) \\\\ P(Y=y) & ={f}_{Y}(y)={\\sum }_{x}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y(y)$ are the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nIn this case, the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Let's add these row and column totals to our table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f_X$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.25}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.35$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.5}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.05$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.25}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f_Y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$0.35$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$0.65$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe marginal mass function for $X$ is given in the following table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f_X(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.25$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, using the table of values of the marginal mass function for $X,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =P(X\\in \\{2,3\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =0.5+0.25 \\\\  & =0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161872 1074 "If the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have joint distribution with joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & ={f}_{X}(x)={\\sum }_{y}f(x,y) \\\\ P(Y=y) & ={f}_{Y}(y)={\\sum }_{x}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y(y)$ are the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nIn this case, the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Let's add these row and column totals to our table:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.25}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.35$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.5}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.25}$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $0.35$ | $0.65$ | $1$ |\n\nThe marginal mass function for $X$ is given in the following table:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f_X(x)$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ | $0.25$ |\n\nFinally, using the table of values of the marginal mass function for $X,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =P(X\\in \\{2,3\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =0.5+0.25 \\\\  & =0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139540364 17592186576411 false] [17592186161873 1071 "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.05$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.25$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.05$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.15$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nCompute $P\\Big( (X,Y) \\in \\