ter\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}44}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161883 1074 "Under the null hypothesis, we have that $X\\sim U\\{0,1,2,3,4\\}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=\\dfrac 15,\\qquad x=0,1,2,3,4\n$$\n\nThe sample size is\n\n$$\nN = 45+47+46+42+40 = 220\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected frequencies for each observation are as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {E}_{1} & \\displaystyle  = N \\cdot  P ( X = 0 ) = 220 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{5}} = 44 \\\\ \\displaystyle {E}_{2} & \\displaystyle  = N \\cdot  P ( X = 2 ) = 220 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{5}} = 44 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\phantom{=} \\vdots  \\\\ \\displaystyle {E}_{5} & \\displaystyle  = N \\cdot  P ( X = 4 ) = 220 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{5}} = 44\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nA table summarizing the observed and corresponding expected frequencies is given below:\n\n| $x_i$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $O_i$ | $45$ | $47$ | $46$ | $42$ | $40$ |\n| $E_i$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}44}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}44}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}44}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}44}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}44}$ |" 13194139540364 17592186576411 false] [17592186161884 1071 "A basketball player practices free throws, shooting $5$ times per session and tracking the number of successful shots. The frequency distribution of successful shots per session is summarized below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Successful Shots $(y_i)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Observed Frequencies $(O_i)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$32$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$67$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$52$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$27$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe player's coach wants to test whether a binomial distribution can model the observed data.\nCarry out a chi-square goodness-of-fit test at the $10\\%$ significance level with the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\:$ The observed distribution can be modeled as $B(5, p)$\n- $H_1:\\:$ The observed distribution cannot be modeled as $B(5, p)$\n\nThe table below gives the values of $w$ that satisfy $P(W\\geq w) = q$, where $W\\sim \\chi^2(\\nu)$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\nu \\setminus q$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.010$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.100$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$3$</td>\n<td>$\\,11.345\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,7.815\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,6.251\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$4$</td>\n<td>$\\,13.277\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,9.488\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,7.779\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$5$</td>\n<td>$\\,15.086\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,11.070\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,9.236\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161884 1071 "A basketball player practices free throws, shooting $5$ times per session and tracking the number of successful shots. The frequency distribution of successful shots per session is summarized below.\n\n| Successful Shots $(y_i)$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Observed Frequencies $(O_i)$ | $12$ | $32$ | $67$ | $52$ | $27$ | $10$ |\n\nThe player's coach wants to test whether a binomial distribution can model the observed data.\nCarry out a chi-square goodness-of-fit test at the $10\\%$ significance level with the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\:$ The observed distribution can be modeled as $B(5, p)$\n- $H_1:\\:$ The observed distribution cannot be modeled as $B(5, p)$\n\nThe table below gives the values of $w$ that satisfy $P(W\\geq w) = q$, where $W\\sim \\chi^2(\\nu)$.\n\n| $\\nu \\setminus q$ | $0.010$ | $0.050$ | $0.100$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $3$ | $\\,11.345\\,$ | $\\,7.815\\,$ | $\\,6.251\\,$ |\n| $4$ | $\\,13.277\\,$ | $\\,9.488\\,$ | $\\,7.779\\,$ |\n| $5$ | $\\,15.086\\,$ | $\\,11.070\\,$ | $\\,9.236\\,$ |" 13194139540364 17592186576411 false] [17592186161884 1074 "Suppose we have a set of observed frequencies $O_i$ and corresponding expected frequencies $E_i$, as defined by a theoretical model under the null hypothesis. Then, the random variable\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i} \\frac{(O_i - E_i)^2}{E_i}\n$$\n\napproximately follows a chi-square distribution with $\\nu$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $O_i$ is the observed frequency for category $i$, and\n- $E_i$ is the expected frequency for category $i$, as predicted under the null hypothesis.\n\nThis approximation holds when all expected frequencies $E_i$ are sufficiently large (typically\n\n$$\nE_i \\geq 5\n$$\n\nfor each category), and the observations are independent.\n\nThe number of observations is\n\n$$\nN= 12+32+67+52+27+10 = 200\n$$\n\nWe can calculate an estimate of $p$, which we denote as $\\hat p$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hat{p} &= \\frac{total number of successes}{total number of trials} \\\\\n&= (∑(y_{i} \\cdot O_{i}))/(n \\cdot N)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N$ is the number of observations, and $n$ is the number of trials in each binomial sequence. So, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hat{p} &= \\frac{0 \\cdot 12 + 1 \\cdot 32 + 2 \\cdot 67 + 3 \\cdot 52 + 4 \\cdot 27 + 5 \\cdot 0}{5 \\cdot 200} \\\\\n&= 0.48\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the proposed distribution is $Y\\sim B(5, \\, 0.48)$.\n\nUnder the hypothesis that $Y\\sim B(5, \\, 0.48)$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle P ( Y = y ) & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{y} \\right) {p}^{y} ( 1 - p {)}^{n - y} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{y} \\right) ( 0.48 {)}^{y} ( 0.52 {)}^{5 - y} , \\hspace{2em} y = 0 , 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 , 5\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate the probability of each value of $Y{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle P ( Y = 0 ) & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0} \\right) ( 0.48 {)}^{0} ( 0.52 {)}^{5} = 0.038 \\\\ \\displaystyle P ( Y = 1 ) & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1} \\right) ( 0.48 {)}^{1} ( 0.52 {)}^{4} = 0.1755 \\\\ \\displaystyle P ( Y = 2 ) & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2} \\right) ( 0.48 {)}^{2} ( 0.52 {)}^{3} = 0.324 \\\\ \\displaystyle P ( Y = 3 ) & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{3} \\right) ( 0.48 {)}^{3} ( 0.52 {)}^{2} = 0.299 \\\\ \\displaystyle P ( Y = 4 ) & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{4} \\right) ( 0.48 {)}^{4} ( 0.52 {)}^{1} = 0.138 \\\\ \\displaystyle P ( Y = 5 ) & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{5} \\right) ( 0.48 {)}^{5} ( 0.52 {)}^{0} = 0.0255\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected frequencies for each observation are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{0} &= N \\cdot P(Y = 0) = 200 \\cdot 0.0380 = 7.6 \\\\\nE_{1} &= N \\cdot P(Y = 1) = 200 \\cdot 0.1755 = 35.1 \\\\\nE_{2} &= N \\cdot P(Y = 2) = 200 \\cdot 0.3240 = 64.8 \\\\\nE_{3} &= N \\cdot P(Y = 3) = 200 \\cdot 0.2990 = 59.8 \\\\\nE_{4} &= N \\cdot P(Y = 4) = 200 \\cdot 0.1380 = 27.6 \\\\\nE_{5} &= N \\cdot P(Y = 5) = 200 \\cdot 0.0255 = 5.1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA table summarizing the observed and corresponding theoretical frequencies is given below:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_i$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$O_i$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$32$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$67$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$52$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$27$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$E_i$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7.6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$35.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$64.8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$59.8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$27.6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5.1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\dfrac{(O_i-E_i)^2}{E_i}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.5474$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2738$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.0747$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.0174$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.0130$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4.7078$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe have rounded values to four decimal places, where appropriate. Notice that all of the expected frequencies are greater than or equal to $5$.\n\nFirst, we compute our test statistic by summing the last row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(i)((O_{i} - E_{i})^{2})/(E_{i}) &= 2.5474 + 0.2738 + 0.0747 + 1.0173 + 0.0130 + 4.7078 \\\\\n&\\approx 8.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the test statistic is approximately $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}8.6}$.\n\nThere are two constraints on our data: the total number of observations must equal $N$, and our estimated probability of success $\\hat p$. Therefore, the number of degrees of freedom $\\nu$ is calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nν &= number of categories - number of constraints \\\\\n&= 6 - 2 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are\n\n$$\n\\nu = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\ndegrees of freedom.\n\nFrom the given chi-square table, the critical value for $\\nu=4$ at a $10\\%$ significance level is\n\n$$\n\\chi^2_{\\textrm{critical}}=7.779\n$$\n\nand the critical region is\n\n$$\nX\\geq 7.779\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/39/3949c36fa794d40d277708c4ac5808c5895fa40c327445f1fbeb99af7bd136e2.png)\n\nOur test statistic $(8.6)$ lies inside the critical region. Therefore, there is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{sufficient}}$ evidence to reject the null hypothesis." 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161884 1074 "Suppose we have a set of observed frequencies $O_i$ and corresponding expected frequencies $E_i$, as defined by a theoretical model under the null hypothesis. Then, the random variable\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i} \\frac{(O_i - E_i)^2}{E_i}\n$$\n\napproximately follows a chi-square distribution with $\\nu$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $O_i$ is the observed frequency for category $i$, and\n- $E_i$ is the expected frequency for category $i$, as predicted under the null hypothesis.\n\nThis approximation holds when all expected frequencies $E_i$ are suffi