.779\n$$\n\nand the critical region is\n\n$$\nX\\geq 7.779\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/39/3949c36fa794d40d277708c4ac5808c5895fa40c327445f1fbeb99af7bd136e2.png)\n\nOur test statistic $(8.6)$ lies inside the critical region. Therefore, there is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{sufficient}}$ evidence to reject the null hypothesis." 13194139540364 17592186576411 false] [17592186161885 1071 "Suppose $Y$ is a random variable with support $S = \\{0,1,2,3\\}$. A random sample is carried out, and the sample data is shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Observations $(y_i)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Observed Frequencies $(O_i)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$27$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nCarry out a chi-square goodness of fit test at the $1\\%$ significance level with the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\:$ The observed distribution can be modeled as $B(3,0.6)$\n- $H_1:\\:$ The observed distribution cannot be modeled as $B(3,0.6)$\n\nThe table below gives the values of $w$ that satisfy $P(W\\geq w) = q$, where $W\\sim \\chi^2(\\nu)$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\nu \\setminus q$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.010$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.100$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,6.635\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,3.841\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,2.706\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,9.210\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,5.991\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,4.605\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,11.345\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,7.815\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,6.251\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161885 1071 "Suppose $Y$ is a random variable with support $S = \\{0,1,2,3\\}$. A random sample is carried out, and the sample data is shown below.\n\n| Observations $(y_i)$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Observed Frequencies $(O_i)$ | $3$ | $12$ | $27$ | $8$ |\n\nCarry out a chi-square goodness of fit test at the $1\\%$ significance level with the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\:$ The observed distribution can be modeled as $B(3,0.6)$\n- $H_1:\\:$ The observed distribution cannot be modeled as $B(3,0.6)$\n\nThe table below gives the values of $w$ that satisfy $P(W\\geq w) = q$, where $W\\sim \\chi^2(\\nu)$.\n\n| $\\nu \\setminus q$ | $0.010$ | $0.050$ | $0.100$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $\\,6.635\\,$ | $\\,3.841\\,$ | $\\,2.706\\,$ |\n| $2$ | $\\,9.210\\,$ | $\\,5.991\\,$ | $\\,4.605\\,$ |\n| $3$ | $\\,11.345\\,$ | $\\,7.815\\,$ | $\\,6.251\\,$ |" 13194139540364 17592186576411 false] [17592186161885 1074 "Suppose we have a set of observed frequencies $O_i$ and corresponding expected frequencies $E_i$, as defined by a theoretical model under the null hypothesis. Then, the random variable\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i} \\frac{(O_i - E_i)^2}{E_i}\n$$\n\napproximately follows a chi-square distribution with $\\nu$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $O_i$ is the observed frequency for category $i$, and\n- $E_i$ is the expected frequency for category $i$, as predicted under the null hypothesis.\n\nThis approximation holds when all expected frequencies $E_i$ are sufficiently large (typically\n\n$$\nE_i \\geq 5\n$$\n\nfor each category), and the observations are independent.\n\nThe number of observations is\n\n$$\nN= 3+12+27+8 = 50\n$$\n\nUnder the hypothesis that $Y\\sim B(3, \\, 0.6)$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle P ( Y = y ) & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{y} \\right) {p}^{y} ( 1 - p {)}^{n - y} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{y} \\right) ( 0.6 {)}^{y} ( 0.4 {)}^{3 - y} , \\hspace{2em} y = 0 , 1 , 2 , 3.\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate the probability of each value of $Y{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle P ( Y = 0 ) & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{0} \\right) ( 0.6 {)}^{0} ( 0.4 {)}^{3} = 0.064 \\\\ \\displaystyle P ( Y = 1 ) & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{1} \\right) ( 0.6 {)}^{1} ( 0.4 {)}^{2} = 0.288 \\\\ \\displaystyle P ( Y = 2 ) & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{2} \\right) ( 0.6 {)}^{2} ( 0.4 {)}^{1} = 0.432 \\\\ \\displaystyle P ( Y = 3 ) & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{3} \\right) ( 0.6 {)}^{3} ( 0.4 {)}^{0} = 0.216\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected frequencies for each observation are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{0} &= N \\cdot P(Y = 0) = 50 \\cdot 0.064 = 3.2 \\\\\nE_{1} &= N \\cdot P(Y = 1) = 50 \\cdot 0.288 = 14.4 \\\\\nE_{2} &= N \\cdot P(Y = 2) = 50 \\cdot 0.432 = 21.6 \\\\\nE_{3} &= N \\cdot P(Y = 3) = 50 \\cdot 0.216 = 10.8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that there is one frequency less than $5$, so we combine the values $Y=0$ and $Y=1$.\n\nA table summarizing the observed and corresponding theoretical frequencies is given below:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_i$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\leq 1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$O_i$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$27$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$E_i$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17.6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21.6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10.8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\dfrac{(O_i-E_i)^2}{E_i}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.384\\,091$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.35$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.725\\,926$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe have rounded values to six decimal places, where appropriate. Notice that all of the expected frequencies here are greater than or equal to $5$.\n\nFirst, we compute our test statistic by summing the last row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(i)((O_{i} - E_{i})^{2})/(E_{i}) &= 0.384091 + 1.35 + 0.725926 \\\\\n&\\approx 2.46\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the test statistic is approximately $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2.46}$.\n\nThere is one constraint on our data: the total number of observations must equal $N$. Therefore, the number of degrees of freedom $\\nu$ is calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nν &= number of categories - number of constraints \\\\\n&= 3 - 1 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** We must use the number of categories in the combined table!\n\nSo, there are\n\n$$\n\\nu = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\ndegrees of freedom.\n\nFrom the given chi-square table, the critical value for $\\nu=1$ at a $1\\%$ significance level is\n\n$$\n\\chi^2_{\\textrm{critical}}=9.210\n$$\n\nand the critical region is\n\n$$\nX\\geq 9.210\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/29/29977e662d295a3aee4554f405d52eed72c63f768c1ee40bf14e7bd0d5a91544.png)\n\nOur test statistic $(2.46)$ does not lie inside the critical region. Therefore, there is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{insufficient}}$ evidence to reject the null hypothesis." 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161885 1074 "Suppose we have a set of observed frequencies $O_i$ and corresponding expected frequencies $E_i$, as defined by a theoretical model under the null hypothesis. Then, the random variable\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i} \\frac{(O_i - E_i)^2}{E_i}\n$$\n\napproximately follows a chi-square distribution with $\\nu$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $O_i$ is the observed frequency for category $i$, and\n- $E_i$ is the expected frequency for category $i$, as predicted under the null hypothesis.\n\nThis approximation holds when all expected frequencies $E_i$ are sufficiently large (typically\n\n$$\nE_i \\geq 5\n$$\n\nfor each category), and the observations are independent.\n\nThe number of observations is\n\n$$\nN= 3+12+27+8 = 50\n$$\n\nUnder the hypothesis that $Y\\sim B(3, \\, 0.6)$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle P ( Y = y ) & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{y} \\right) {p}^{y} ( 1 - p {)}^{n - y} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{y} \\right) ( 0.6 {)}^{y} ( 0.4 {)}^{3 - y} , \\hspace{2em} y = 0 , 1 , 2 , 3.\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate the probability of each value of $Y{:}$\n\n$