3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}17.1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}19.4}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161891 1074 "Under the null hypothesis, we have that $X\\sim \\mathrm{Po}(2.5)$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = x) &= \\frac{λ^{x}e^{- λ}}{x!} \\\\\n&= ((2.5)^{x}e^{-2.5})/(x!), x = 0, 1, 2, …\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate the probability of each missing value of $X{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = 0) &= ((2.5)^{0}e^{-2.5})/(0!) \\approx 0.082085 \\\\\nP(X = 1) &= ((2.5)^{1}e^{-2.5})/(1!) \\approx 0.205212 \\\\\nP(X = 2) &= ((2.5)^{2}e^{-2.5})/(2!) \\approx 0.256516 \\\\\nP(X = 3) &= ((2.5)^{3}e^{-2.5})/(3!) \\approx 0.213763 \\\\\nP(X \\ge 4) &= 1 - P(X = 0) - P(X = 1) - P(X = 2) - P(X = 3) = 0.242424\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe number of observations is\n\n$$\nN = 15 + 18 + 9 + 26+ 12 = 80\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected frequencies for each observation are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{0} &= N \\cdot P(X = 0) = 80 \\cdot 0.082085 \\approx 6.6 \\\\\nE_{2} &= N \\cdot P(X = 2) = 80 \\cdot 0.256516 \\approx 20.5 \\\\\nE_{3} &= N \\cdot P(X = 3) = 80 \\cdot 0.213763 \\approx 17.1 \\\\\nE_{4} &= N \\cdot P(X \\ge 4) = 80 \\cdot 0.242424 \\approx 19.4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA table summarizing the observed and corresponding expected frequencies is given below:\n\n| $x_i$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $\\geq 4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $O_i$ | $15$ | $18$ | $9$ | $26$ | $12$ |\n| $E_i$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}6.6}$ | $16.4$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}20.5}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}17.1}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}19.4}$ |" 13194139540364 17592186576411 false] [17592186161892 1071 "Suppose $Y$ is a continuous random variable with support $S=[0,120]$. A random sample is carried out, and the frequency distribution of the sample data is shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Class</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0\\leq y_i < 30$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$30\\leq y_i< 90$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$90\\leq y_i \\leq 120$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Observed Frequency $(O_i)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$31$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$42$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$27$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nA chi-square goodness of fit test is conducted at the $1\\%$ significance level with the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\: Y\\sim U[0,120]$\n- $H_1:\\:Y\\not\\sim U[0,120]$\n\nCalculate the value of the test statistic for this hypothesis test." 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161892 1071 "Suppose $Y$ is a continuous random variable with support $S=[0,120]$. A random sample is carried out, and the frequency distribution of the sample data is shown below.\n\n| Class | $0\\leq y_i < 30$ | $30\\leq y_i< 90$ | $90\\leq y_i \\leq 120$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Observed Frequency $(O_i)$ | $31$ | $42$ | $27$ |\n\nA chi-square goodness of fit test is conducted at the $1\\%$ significance level with the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\: Y\\sim U[0,120]$\n- $H_1:\\:Y\\not\\sim U[0,120]$\n\nCalculate the value of the test statistic for this hypothesis test." 13194139540364 17592186576411 false] [17592186161892 1074 "Suppose we have a set of observed frequencies $O_i$ and corresponding expected frequencies $E_i$, as defined by a theoretical model under the null hypothesis. Then, the random variable\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i} \\frac{(O_i - E_i)^2}{E_i}\n$$\n\napproximately follows a chi-square distribution with $\\nu$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $O_i$ is the observed frequency for category $i$, and\n- $E_i$ is the expected frequency for category $i$, as predicted under the null hypothesis.\n\nThis approximation holds when all expected frequencies $E_i$ are sufficiently large (typically\n\n$$\nE_i \\geq 5\n$$\n\nfor each category), and the observations are independent.\n\nUnder the null hypothesis, we have that $Y\\sim U[0,120]$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(a\\leq Y\\lt b)= \\dfrac{b-a}{120-0} = \\dfrac{b-a}{120}\n$$\n\nwhere $a \\lt b$ and $a,b\\in [0,120]$.\n\nThe sample size is\n\n$$\nN = 31 + 42 + 27 = 100\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected frequencies for each class of observation are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{1} &= N \\cdot P(0 \\le Y < 30) \\\\\n&= 100 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{30 - 0}{120}\\right) \\\\\n&= 25 \\\\\nE_{2} &= N \\cdot P(30 \\le Y < 90) \\\\\n&= 100 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{90 - 30}{120}\\right) \\\\\n&= 50 \\\\\nE_{3} &= N \\cdot P(90 \\le Y < 120) \\\\\n&= 100 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{120 - 90}{120}\\right) \\\\\n&= 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA table summarizing the observed and corresponding expected frequencies is given below:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Class</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0\\leq y_i < 30$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$30\\leq y_i< 90$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$90\\leq y_i \\leq 120$</td>\n</tr><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$O_i$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$31$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$42$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$27$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$E_i$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$50$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\dfrac{(O_i-E_i)^2}{E_i}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.44$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.28$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.16$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe compute our test statistic by summing the last row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(i)((O_{i} - E_{i})^{2})/(E_{i}) &= 1.44 + 1.28 + 0.16 \\\\\n&= 2.88\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of the test statistic is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2.88}$." 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161892 1074 "Suppose we have a set of observed frequencies $O_i$ and corresponding expected frequencies $E_i$, as defined by a theoretical model under the null hypothesis. Then, the random variable\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i} \\frac{(O_i - E_i)^2}{E_i}\n$$\n\napproximately follows a chi-square distribution with $\\nu$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $O_i$ is the observed frequency for category $i$, and\n- $E_i$ is the expected frequency for category $i$, as predicted under the null hypothesis.\n\nThis approximation holds when all expected frequencies $E_i$ are sufficiently large (typically\n\n$$\nE_i \\geq 5\n$$\n\nfor each category), and the observations are independent.\n\nUnder the null hypothesis, we have that $Y\\sim U[0,120]$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(a\\leq Y\\lt b)= \\dfrac{b-a}{120-0} = \\dfrac{b-a}{120}\n$$\n\nwhere $a \\lt b$ and $a,b\\in [0,120]$.\n\nThe sample size is\n\n$$\nN = 31 + 42 + 27 = 100\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected frequencies for each class of observation are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{1} &= N \\cdot P(0 \\le Y < 30) \\\\\n&= 100 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{30 - 0}{120}\\right) \\\\\n&= 25 \\\\\nE_{2} &= N \\cdot P(30 \\le Y < 90) \\\\\n&= 100 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{90 - 30}{120}\\right) \\\\\n&= 50 \\\\\nE_{3} &= N \\cdot P(90 \\le Y < 120) \\\\\n&= 100 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{120 - 90}{120}\\right) \\\\\n&= 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA table summarizing the observed and corresponding expected frequencies is given below:\n\n| Class | $0\\leq y_i < 30$ | $30\\leq y_i< 90$ | $90\\leq y_i \\leq 120$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $O_i$ | $31$ | $42$ | $27$ |\n| $E_i$ | $25$ | $50$ | $25$ |\n| $\\dfrac{(O_i-E_i)^2}{E_i}$ | $1.44$ | $1.28$ | $0.16$ |\n\nWe compute our test statistic by summing the last row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(i)((O_{i} - E_{i})^{2})/(E_{i}) &= 1.44 + 1.28 + 0.16 \\\\\n&= 2.88\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of the test statistic is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2.88}$." 13194139540364 17592186576411 false] [17592186161893 1071 "A food quality control team tests boxes of packaged salads to check for damage. Each box contains $3$ salad packs. The results of the test are summarized below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Number of Damaged Packs in a Box $(x_i)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency $(O_i)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$30$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nA chi-square goodness-of-fit test is conducted at the $1\\%$ significance level with the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\:$ The sample data fit a binomial distribution $B(3,p)$\n- $H_1:\\:$ The sample data does not fit a binomial distribution $B(3,p)$\n\nUse this data to calculate an estimate of $p$." 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161893 1071 "A food quality control team tests boxes of packaged salads to check for damage. Each box contains $3$ salad packs. The results of the test are summarized below.\n\n| Number of Damaged Packs in a Box $(x_i)$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Frequency $(O_i)$ | $20$ | $30$ | $8$ | $2$ |\n\nA chi-square goodness-of-fit test is conducted at the $1\\%$ significance level with the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\:$ The sample data fit a binomial distribution $B(3,p)$\n- $H_1:\\:$ The sample data does not fit a binomial distribution $B(3,p)$\n\nUse this data to calculate an estimate of $p$." 13194139540364 17592186576411 false] [17592186161899 1074 "We can write down the binary fraction using a place value chart:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"><strong>Place Values</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$2^1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$2^0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">.</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$2^{-1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$2^{-2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$2^{-3}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$2^{-4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"><strong>Digits</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">.</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, our number in base $10$ can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(10.0101)_{2} &= \\overbrace{1 \\cdot 2^{1} + 1 \\cdot 2^{0}}^{\\text{whole}} + \\overbrace{1 \\cdot 2^{-1} + 1 \\cdot 2^{-2} + 0 \\cdot 2^{-3} + 1 \\cdot 2^{-4}}^{\\text{fraction}} \\\\\n&= 2 + 1 + 0.5 + 0.25 + 0 + 0.0625 \\\\\n&= (3.8125)_{10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161899 1074 "We can write down the binary fraction using a place value chart:\n\n| **Place Values** | $2^1$ | $2^0$ | . | $2^{-1}$ | $2^{-2}$ | $2^{-3}$ | $2^{-4}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Digits** | $1$ | $1$ | . | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, our number in base $10$ can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(10.0101)_{2} &= \\overbrace{1 \\cdot 2^{1} + 1 \\cdot 2^{0}}^{\\text{whole}} + \\overbrace{1 \\cdot 2^{-1} + 1 \\cdot 2^{-2} + 0 \\cdot 2^{-3} + 1 \\cdot 2^{-4}}^{\\text{fraction}} \\\\\n&= 2 + 1 + 0.5 + 0.25 + 0 + 0.0625 \\\\\n&= (3.8125)_{10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139540364 17592186576411 false] [17592186161900 1074 "We express the number $(3.25)_{10}$ in base $10$ as follows:\n\n- For the whole number part ($3$), we extract the powers of $2$ from the number, starting from the largest possible one.\n- For the fractional part, we extract the *negative* powers of $2$ from the number, starting from the largest possible one.\n\nTherefore, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3.25 &= \\overbrace{3}^{\\text{whole}} + \\overbrace{0.25}^{\\text{fraction}} \\\\\n&= 2^{1} + 2^{0} + 2^{-2} \\\\\n&= \\underbrace{1 \\cdot 2^{1} + 1 \\cdot 2^{0}}_{\\text{whole}} + \\underbrace{0 \\cdot 2^{-1} + 1 \\cdot 2^{-2}}_{\\text{fraction}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the corresponding place value chart is as follows:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"><strong>Place Values</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$2^{1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$2^{0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">.</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$2^{-1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$2^{-2}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"><strong>Digits</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">.</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n(3.25)_{10} = (11.01)_2\n$$" 13194139540364 17592186576411 true] [17592186161900 1074 "We express the number $(3.25)_{10}$ in base $10$ as follows:\n\n- For the whole number part ($3$), we extract the powers of $2$ from the number, starting from the largest possible one.\n- For the fractional part, we extract the *negative* powers of $2$ from the number, starting from the largest possible one.\n\nTherefore, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3.25 &= \\overbrace{3}^{\\text{whole}} + \\overbrace{0.25}^{\\text{fraction}} \\\\\n&= 2^{1} + 2^{0} + 2^{-2} \\\\\n&= \\underbrace{1 \\cdot 2^{1} + 1 \\cdot 2^{0}}_{\\text{whole}} + \\underbrace{0 \\cdot 2^{-1} + 1 \\cdot 2^{-2}}_{\\text{fraction}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the corresponding place value chart is as follows:\n\n| **Place Values** | $2^{1}$ | $2^{0}$ | . | $2^{-1}$ | $2^{-2}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Digits** | $1$ | $1$ | . | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n(3.25)_{10} = (11.01)_2\n$$" 13194139540364 17592186576411 false] [17592186161901 1074 "We extract the negative powers of $2$ from the number, starting from the largest possible one:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.9 &= \\underbrace{0.5}_{1/2} + 0.4 \\\\\n&= \\underbrace{0.5}_{1/2} + \\underbrace{0.25}_{1/4} + 0.15 \\\\\n&= \\underbrace{0.5}_{1/2} + \\underbrace{0.25}_{1/4} + \\underbrace{0.125}_{1/8} + 0.025 \\\\\n&= \\under